#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "8cc1617d-ba3f-5ae3-aca0-90354ffbd62f" :question/math-academy-id "e-4273" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is directed due north-east and $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=11$. The vector $\\mathbf{b}$ is directed due south-east and $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=8$. Find the exact value of $\\mid \\mathbf{a}+\\textbf{b} \\mid$." :question/worked-solution "We use the triangle law of addition to find the sum $\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$:\n\n![](images/a1/a122c2a1146f27a089f991f7e0bd07e4180ca4622bfc95a6b85afc83f339c11d.png)\n\nNotice that we obtain a right triangle. So, we can use Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a + b & ^{2} &= & a & ^{2} +\\left.\\end{vmatrix}b\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&= 11^{2} + 8^{2} \\\\\n&= 185\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a}+\\textbf{b} &= \\sqrt{185}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096645 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c145c97c-c313-597f-b84e-8adb3bdf9c14" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Law of Cosines"}] :question/id #uuid "f0c94cce-790d-50b0-bd9b-13b64867848b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4275" :question/problem "Consider the diagram shown above. If $\\mid \\mathbf{a}|=4$ and $\\mid \\mathbf{b}|=3$, Find the exact value of$\\mid \\mathbf{a-b} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/92/922fa6572855e2a40f2b0fdd97bbbc5d6ddf2323228d99d5ab74a7aa7c2072f8.png)" :question/worked-solution "We can interpret the subtraction\n$\\mid \\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b} \\mid$\nas the addition\n$\\mid \\mathbf{a} + (-\\mathbf{b}) \\mid$.\n\nThe vector $-\\mathbf{b}$ has the same magnitude as $\\mathbf{b}$ and the direction that is opposite to $\\mathbf{b}$. We use the triangle law of addition to find $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\mathbf{b}$. The resultant goes from the start of $\\mathbf{a}$ to the head of $-\\mathbf{b}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b1cd51e96439a8e3317fec12c9d1e57aa06bc9a99680849990e853d3ce6dbf7.png)\n\nSo, we can use the law of cosines as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}a - b & ^{2} &= \\end{vmatrix}a + \\left.(-b)\\right|^{2} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}a & ^{2} + & -b & ^{2} - 2 \\cdot & a & \\cdot & -b\\end{vmatrix}\\cdot \\cos (180^{∘} - 120^{∘}) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}a & ^{2} + & b & ^{2} - 2 & a\\end{vmatrix} \\mid b \\mid \\cos (60^{∘}) \\\\\n&= 3^{2} + 4^{2} - 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&= 9 + 16 - 12 \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid \\mathbf{a-b} &= \\sqrt{13}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096485 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096690 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e0146b8-421b-5d63-a933-3de9bcaa7ab2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Magnitude of a Vector From a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "ba160db3-7799-5a25-8c50-5147100f7475" :question/math-academy-id "e-5013" :question/problem "Consider the picture shown below. Find the exact value of $\\mid \\mathbf{a} \\mid$.\n\n![](images/14/140b4f8ced622eaf505d97c0f4d9c029365536927ea0c3e38e76904cba84ace2.png)" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1f/1fc7b5efcaa81dd1a3416757c976e5d17b96f5040069b5eeeb941b1f7c149c34.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have\n\n$$\n\\angle AHB = 90^\\circ\n$$\n\n,\n\n$$\nAH=35\\:\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nBH=25\\:\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nSo, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^{2} + BH^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{35^{2} + 25^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1850} \\\\\n&= 5\\sqrt{74}mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "750024aa-db50-5f98-8404-f6e69c56d142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571076} {:answer/id #uuid "bfa0a12f-cda8-5fd5-abe7-5e30e71cce8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571189} {:answer/id #uuid "c932bcf2-cafc-5b21-8eb5-b04c461de955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571201} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e0b7d3-574e-5e58-ac51-e9d34b36fed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571220} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d71863-c6cb-51c1-9392-5d2245d50ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfa0a12f-cda8-5fd5-abe7-5e30e71cce8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571189} :answer-field/id #uuid "884db0bd-f9ab-5fb9-9f2d-26d5107b8c4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9347b09d-cd71-571e-b9c7-6616a5a13bf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118912" :question/problem "In rectangle $ABCD$, $AB=18$ and $BC=9.$ Find $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/3e/3e03c8d3377aef27d7542ef2d734ebd64d00bbc1539e63222b7df9abc696bd44.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, in $△BAD$ we have $∠BAD={90}^{∘}$, $AB=18$ and $AD=BC=9.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{BD}| & =BD \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}+A{D}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{18}^{2}+{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{405} \\\\  & =9\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|=9\\sqrt{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10762df6-57f0-5b84-885e-274abb0ee789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10762df6-57f0-5b84-885e-274abb0ee789" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{65}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570914} :answer-field/id #uuid "6efd4c2e-cb0c-5f1d-89f9-33793724f1d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5fdbf2c-dec8-5273-bdaa-1e13a6c2ba92" :question/math-academy-id "q-316812" :question/problem "![](images/08/08ac5be11bc156cf1e43fc7d5e6283b4c4514c78d3280180f8d66eca8c66eab6.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $a$ is perpendicular to $b$, $|a|=4$ and $|b|=7.$ Find the **exact** value of $|a+b|.$\n\n$|a+b|=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a right triangle. So, we can use Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={4}^{2}+{7}^{2} \\\\  & =65\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|a+b|=\\sqrt{65}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aaa42a66-fbd7-53ab-a6bd-9c97d8cc6552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aaa42a66-fbd7-53ab-a6bd-9c97d8cc6552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571158} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaaafb85-4fd1-52eb-a622-74ba4be6a38d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47914265-044d-5f89-bb50-8eabdded7a08" :question/math-academy-id "q-316898" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a67039e1192670e638fbaf88814a6474c9262dee32117d4cdb48db0f0d904fb.png)\n\nConsider the diagram above, where $a$ is perpendicular to $b$, $|a|=12$ and $|b|=9.$ Find the **exact** value of $|a+b|.$\n\n$|a+b|=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we have a right triangle. So, we can use Pythagorean theorem as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{|a+b|}^{2} & ={|a|}^{2}+{|b|}^{2} \\\\  & ={12}^{2}+{9}^{2} \\\\  & =144+81 \\\\  & =225\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|a+b|=\\sqrt{225}=15.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186571436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48579a8d-61f0-50c1-81f6-7b087b3f936c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Sum of Vectors Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "360a01a5-0fde-5f9a-bcdd-05b77ca562f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "360a01a5-0fde-5f9a-bcdd-05b77ca562f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{43}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570974} :answer-field/id #uuid "9362a981-d476-5fb3-b9f4-48cf3184521d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186571338}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1c67635-2334-5362-a331-e8281f5f234e" :question/math-academy-id "q-316909" :question/problem "In rectangle $ABCD,$ $AB=10$ and $BC=6\\sqrt{2}.$ Find the **exact** value of $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|.$\n\n$|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|=$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/53/537669ec91bb6fd620d64816d4ccf0e0a5c403aa8ba6e03c8fb89c8d71785d24.png)\n\nAs we can see from the picture above, in $△BAD$ we have $m∠BAD={90}^{∘},$ $AB=10,$ and $AD=BC=6\\sqrt{2}.$ So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{\\to }{BD}| & =BD \\\\  & =\\sqrt{A{B}^{2}+A{D}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{10}^{2}+(6\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{100+72} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{172} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{43}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $|\\overset{\\to }{BD}|=2\\sqrt{43}.$"}]}
